			<div class="content" id="post-content-2762941">
									

<p>Couple minor things:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>applied to a currently living organism</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd cut 'currently', it reads like you're saying the organisms have to be alive <em>right now</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>now known as SCP-2891-1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What were they known as before? This was immersion-breaking. I'd change this to something else.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>although the average number</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The average number of land? Just say 'average area'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the designation of objects</p>
</blockquote>
<p>for, not of</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2891-2 is the designation of objects and structures formed by SCP-2891 around the affected area of SCP-2891-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd end this at 'formed by SCP-2891'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>found to manifest at varying times in the stages of SCP-2891</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But&#8230;in the stages, they only appear in one stage.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>in order to appear as said officers</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For the second time, I'd cut this. It makes the sentence redundant and speculative. Why attribute motive to something that may be wholly mindless?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>barbed wire increased,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, this makes grammatical sense in the context of the list. Also, sweet jesus the run-on sentence.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Nitpicks aside.</p>
<hr />
<p>While this has seen more revisions, it's still not where it could be. The interview, while no longer full of outdated PL tropes, is still bland straight exposition that gets in the way of the character. Having this neat, sound little backstory of the anomaly presented with a little bow on top sucks out the mystery, and detracts from the potential emotional impact of the character's story.</p>
<p>The second log from the MTF is simply cheap stock gore, that again, detracts from the final portion. I'll add that it also takes away from the imagery for me. You could instead reinforce the picture painted by the (stages) log, and immerse readers in the discovery of Taylor and their tragic situation&#8230;</p>
<p>Why you'd take this from terrifying confinement- playing on autophobic and claustophobic fears- and end up at more 'blood and guts' is beyond me. You had a good thing without that.</p>
<p>I think more focus should be on Dr. Taylor and the correspondences. I'd open that up some and add some dates to them to help cement the forward progression. Good luck.</p>

							</div>

								
					
